If GPS has no valid fix, the system should explicitly mark the location as unavailable rather than treating 0.000000, 0.000000 as a valid position.

6. Neighbor Discovery and Relay Eligibility

TerraLink devices can periodically exchange small HELLO/beacon messages. A node can maintain a temporary neighbor table containing Node ID, battery/availability and measured link quality.

A personal TerraLink device should not forward every packet automatically. It becomes an eligible temporary forwarding node only when conditions are appropriate.

Sufficient battery

Good RSSI/SNR

Acceptable Packet Delivery Ratio (PDR)

Useful progress toward the destination

Available next-hop connectivity

Node availability and mobility

Packet is not already seen

7. Energy Management

The personal device is primarily responsible for its own user. Forwarding another user's traffic must not unnecessarily exhaust its battery.

Normal mode: prioritize the user's own operation.

Relay-eligible mode: temporarily forward when energy and link conditions are suitable.

Low-battery mode: reduce or stop forwarding and preserve energy for the owner.

Emergency mode: prioritize SOS transmission and essential acknowledgement.

Personal devices can use rechargeable batteries with USB/power-bank charging. Dedicated rescue relay units can use larger batteries and, where appropriate, solar or vehicle charging. Solar is an enhancement for long-duration dedicated relay operation, not a requirement for every trekker device.

8. No Nearby TerraLink User

A purely dynamic user-to-user network cannot forward an SOS if the injured trekker has no nearby TerraLink node and no reachable relay path. Dynamic relaying cannot create a communication path where no participating node exists.

TerraLink addresses this by separating opportunistic personal forwarding from dedicated rescue deployment:

Personal devices provide opportunistic forwarding when users are nearby.

Rangers can provide more reliable mobile forwarding.

Rescue/disaster teams can carry dedicated relay units into the affected region.

Dedicated relays can be deployed progressively to extend a path toward an isolated area.

9. Rescue Station Placement

The rescue station is the receiving command/gateway point, not a random relay inside the forest. Suitable locations include a forest control/ranger station, park or trekking base camp, emergency response center, nearby control room, or another accessible location with reliable connectivity.

Dedicated rescue relay units act as mobile extensions of the rescue operation and can be carried or deployed closer to the affected region.

10. Rescue-Team Deployment

During an organized rescue or disaster response, dedicated TerraLink relay units can be carried toward the affected area. These units are designed for forwarding and can use higher-capacity batteries, suitable antennas, rugged packaging, and optional solar/vehicle charging.

Example: Victim → available personal/ranger TerraLink → Dedicated Relay 1 → Dedicated Relay 2 → Rescue Station / command point. The actual number and placement of dedicated relays depends on terrain, link quality, distance and field measurements.

11. Routing Concept

The routing approach should be lightweight and adapted to LoRa's limited data rate, airtime, shared channel and energy constraints. A practical concept is TTL-bounded controlled flooding with duplicate suppression and optional AODV-inspired reverse-path/ACK behavior.

Generate SOS

Check GPS validity

Evaluate neighbors

Select suitable forwarder

Transmit packet

Use ACK/forwarding evidence where implemented

Rediscover a route if the path fails

12. Packet Flooding Prevention

If many nodes receive the same SOS, uncontrolled retransmission can create flooding, congestion, collisions and battery drain.

Unique Packet ID

Source ID

Duplicate-packet cache

TTL / hop limit

Relay eligibility rules

Controlled forwarding

Limited retransmissions

ACK/suppression where implemented

If Node B has already received SOS_1024, a second copy is recognized as a duplicate and discarded rather than forwarded again.

13. Collision Reduction

LoRa uses a shared wireless channel, so simultaneous transmissions can collide. TerraLink cannot claim zero collisions; it reduces their probability and unnecessary airtime.

Controlled forwarding

Random backoff

Duplicate suppression

Limited retransmission

Short SOS packets

Acknowledgement/suppression

Priority for emergency traffic

14. Mobility and Route Recovery

Because personal devices move, a forwarding node can disappear. If A → B → C becomes invalid, the network should detect the failure and search for another suitable next hop, for example A → D → C.

15. GPS Handling

GPS is primarily required at the SOS source. Relay nodes do not need to continuously acquire GPS solely to forward an already-formed SOS packet. This can reduce energy consumption.

The source should verify GPS validity before treating coordinates as confirmed. If no valid fix is available, the packet should clearly indicate unavailable location and the firmware can retry/update according to the implemented design.

